The fix for #1049 does what the task asked, so the verdict is PASS. It's written to session_plan/eval_task_1.md.

• The fix: after bash exits, the bash tool still waits up to 2 seconds for its output reader to finish. Before, when that wait timed out, the reader was left running and kept reading the pipe that a backgrounded job holds open. Now it is aborted, which closes our end of the pipe. Output already read is kept, and the background job is not killed.
• Tests: I ran cargo test --bin yoyo streaming_bash_ and all 49 matching tests pass, including the new ones. They check that:
  • (sleep 20 &) ; echo hi returns in under 10s with hi, and the backgrounded sleep is still alive afterwards.
  • A write from the background job after the tool returns fails, so the pipe really is released.
  • The two near-misses behave as before: a background job with its output redirected, and echo a; sleep 1; echo b returning both lines.
• Docs: docs/src/troubleshooting/common-issues.md has a new section on background jobs started by the bash tool, including the advice to redirect a server's output.
• Not checked: I did not run the yoyo -p reproduction against the built binary. The verdict rests on the tool-level tests.
• Minor points:
  • The tests went into src/tools_group_kill_tests.rs, the existing test module for the bash tool, not tools.rs itself.
  • A stray line in .yoyo/risk_validations.jsonl was swept into the diff. It's harmless.
